# mcp-parity-census allowlist (T-2747) — consumed by scripts/check-mcp-parity-census.sh
#
# One MCP tool per line: `<tool_name>  # <reason>`.
#
# A listed tool is still COUNTED AND REPORTED in the census — it never vanishes from the
# total — it simply does not fire. This is a LEDGER OF AN OPEN QUESTION, in the same
# tradition as the charter-drift allowlist's 28 entries pending T-2548. It is not a
# permanent exemption and it is not a claim that these tools work.
#
# WHAT THE ENTRIES BELOW MEAN. The parity suite asserts 24 of 260 MCP tools (9.2%). The
# remaining 236 have never been compared against their CLI verbs by anything. Before this
# check existed, that fact was invisible: the suite passed, and nothing distinguished
# "this pair was checked and agrees" from "nobody has ever looked at this pair".
#
# Enumerating them here is the point. Freezing today's gap makes it visible and makes it
# a ratchet: a NEW MCP tool added tomorrow appears in neither the covered set nor this
# list, so it fires on the next run and requires a decision. Working the list down is
# T-2748.
#
# HOW TO REMOVE AN ENTRY: add a parity case naming the tool in
# crates/termlink-mcp/tests/parity.rs, then delete its line here. The census moves it from
# acknowledged to covered. Deleting a line WITHOUT adding the assertion re-fires it, which
# is the intended behaviour — there is no way to mark a tool resolved except by asserting it.
#
# Per PL-345 the reason on each line has to be true and complete. "unexamined" is the whole
# truth here: no assertion exists, none has been attempted, and T-2748 tracks the work. It
# is deliberately NOT phrased as a justification — nothing about these tools has been
# reviewed and found acceptable, and an entry implying otherwise would convert an open
# question into a closed one.

